---
name: curried-def-every-spelling
description: "a curried def keeps every parameter clause wherever it is DECLARED — object, class, extension, block-local — and however it is called, including with a trailing using clause"
backends: [int, jvm, js, v2]
---

# `def f(a)(b)` outside the top level

`BUGS.md` `v2-curried-method-call-does-not-flatten-its-clauses` and
`v2-curried-def-with-a-using-clause-cannot-be-called`. `curried-def-clauses` pins the top-level
`def`, and that was the ONLY shape anything pinned. Every other place a def can be declared parsed
its parameter list ONCE and then skipped to the `=`, so the second clause's parameters were never
bound and the body's reference to them became a free global.

**THE OBJECT ROW IS THE ONE THAT LOOKED FINE.** `Calc.add(1)(2)` printed `3` before this case
existed — because F declined the whole file (`GAP: unbound global: (global b)`) and the REFERENCE
front compiled it. `ssc info --front-report` said GAP while the program said 3. That is the same
disguise `curried-def-clauses` documents in its own header, one layer further out: it is not enough
for a curried case to pass, it has to pass ON F.

The class-method and extension rows live in `curried-def-member-methods`: the `js` backend answers
`Method not found` for the first and `not callable: NaN` for the second, so keeping them here would
cost this case a backend for two rows. Every row that CAN run on all four does.

Two declaration sites here, plus the registry that must not move:

| where | site that dropped the clause |
|---|---|
| `object` member | `objDefE1` |
| `def` inside a block | `parseBlockDef1` |
| trailing `using` | `collectUS2` — registered nothing, so no given was injected |

The call side is keyed on the callee NAME rather than on the emitted text. `isCurriedApp` reads
`(app (global f) …)`, which is what a top-level call emits and nothing else does: a class method is
`(prim __method__ …)`, an extension is `(prim __methodOrExt__ …)`, an object member is mangled to
`Obj_m`, a block-local is `(local i)`. One rule over the curried table covers all of them.

```scalascript
object Calc:
  def tri(a: Int)(b: Int)(c: Int): Int = a + b + c
  def add(a: Int)(b: Int): Int = a + b

// Int in, Int out on purpose: `"i" ++ x` reads fine on the ssc lanes and does not typecheck when
// the case is compiled through Scala, and a case about parameter CLAUSES must not fail on string
// concatenation.
trait Show[T]:
  def code(x: T): Int

given showInt: Show[Int] with
  def code(x: Int): Int = x * 10

// THE SHAPES THAT MUST NOT MOVE, because the same registry decides them: one written clause plus
// `using`, a context bound, and a multi-param clause plus `using`.
def one(a: Int)(using s: Show[Int]): Int = s.code(a)
def ctxb[A: Show](a: A): Int = summon[Show[A]].code(a)
def plainU(a: Int, b: Int)(using s: Show[Int]): Int = s.code(a + b)

def outer(): Int =
  def inner(a: Int)(b: Int): Int = a + b
  inner(1)(2)

println(Calc.add(1)(2))
println(Calc.tri(1)(2)(3))
println(outer())
println(one(5))
println(ctxb(7))
println(plainU(1, 2))
```
